      tour: `The final canto of Purgatorio. Beatrice, seven women (the four cardinal and three theological virtues), Statius, and Dante move through the Earthly Paradise. Beatrice will not always be with Dante as she is now — she explains why. Dante asks about the tree and the allegorical drama. She answers with a prophecy and the command to write it down; the man she awaits who will set things right is close to God. Matelda leads Dante to the Eunoë; Statius goes too. They drink; they are restored. They are pure and prepared to leap to the stars. The last line of Purgatorio.`,
      blurb: `The final movement of Purgatorio: Beatrice speaks of the DXV who will restore the Church; Dante is led to the Eunoë and restored. "Pure and prepared to leap to the stars." Purgatorio ends.`,
      summary: [
        `Beatrice walks ahead, speaking to Statius and Dante. She says: I am not always going to walk here with you as I am now. She has a mission; she is leading him to his destination. After that, the mode of guidance changes. She speaks of the allegorical events: the eagle represents Rome's gift to the Church; the fox is heresy; the dragon is Satan or schism. The giant is the king of France. A DXV — perhaps five hundred and fifteen, perhaps a prince — will arrive to restore order.`,
        `She chides Dante: even if his body were stone or bronze, he should be able to hold what she tells him in his memory. She asks him: why does his soul stop at the threshold of each new difficulty? He says: I don't know why my sight is veiled. She says: you have the disease of letting present things guide you — they do not lead to truth. She gives him final instruction.`,
        `Matelda leads Dante to the Eunoë — and Statius comes too. They drink. Dante says only: if there were space in this poem, I would describe the sweet drink that would never have satisfied me. But all the pages set aside for the second canticle are full. The art of the constraint is total. "I came back from the most holy waves remade — pure and prepared to leap to the stars."`,
      ],
      appears: [{ id: 'beatrice', name: 'Beatrice' }],
      themes: [{ slug: 'beatrice', label: 'Beatrice' }, { slug: 'architecture', label: 'Architecture' }],
